# Fody and MEF (Managed Extensibility Framework)

You can use [Fody](https://github.com/Fody/Fody) with the [Managed Extensibility Framework (MEF)](https://github.com/Microsoft/MEF). This provides a way to post-build weaving of MEF attributes to assemble .

Fody is a post-build weaver and works by reading the IL (Intermediate Language) assemblied into the .NET assembly and applying the operations as defined in the weave files. Fody is used as an alternative to conventional ILMerge used in .NET applications for combining assemblies at compile time.

It allows you to use MEF attributes directly from your source code and have them post-compiled weaved into your assemblies. This can drastically reduce the effort of maintaining the weaved part of your projects.

Here's an example on how to use it:

1. Install Fody and Fody.MEF via NuGet package manager console:
   
   ```ps
   Install-Package Fody 
   Install-Package Fody.MEF
   ```
2. Add a `[FodyWeavers]` section to your AssemblyInfo:

```cs
[assembly: Fody.Attributes.WeavingTargets(
    AttributeTargets.Assembly, 
    AttributeTargets.Class | AttributeTargets.Interface | AttributeTargets.Struct, 
    AttributeTargets.Constructor | AttributeTargets.Method
)]
```
3. Define MEF related attributes (e.g., `Export`, `ImportingConstructor`, etc.) in your source code. 

4. Build your project. Fody will apply the weaving operation on the build output assembly.

Here is an example of using MEF:

```cs
// Mark the class as an Export
[Export]--------------------	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last position of x in new array list is smaller than or equal to that in old array +1
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX + 1);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@  new array list count should be greater than or equal to that in old array list +1
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount >= Old_arrListCount);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array list contains x after insertion
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}
```

The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute makes sure the code coverage tool does not generate tests that do not fit the under-test method.
Assumes the index is less than or equal to Count and is non-negative.
Checks that the new and old array list counts after insertion are greater or equal to the old one, new index of x is greater or equal than old index of x(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX),
New_arrListLastIndexOfX should be less or equal to Old_arrListLastIndexOfX + 1.
And, New_arrListContainsX should return true indicating that array lists contain x after insertion.

### Code coverage

By using Pex+Coverage attributes, we can now get a detailed code coverage report. This helps us to identify the lines of code that are not yet covered by our testing, which can be particularly useful for identifying areas of the code base that may not be getting adequately tested.

The example of PexCodeCoverage attribute can be implemented as below:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Validation;
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethodUnderTest]
        [PexCodeCoverage]
        public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            // Act
            arrList.Add(x);
            
	    Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(x));
        }
    }
}
```

This will ensure that you get a detailed report of Code Coverage including the list of methods that are part of the test suite and all the statements that are part of these methods that form the test cases. This way you could see which parts of the code are not covered and where.
      
# Dynamic Analysis
Dynamic Analysis is a more comprehensive technique that allows you to test a much wider variety of scenarios and data inputs. This is particularly useful when the functionality under test is non-deterministic in nature.
  
An example of Dynamic Analysis for the Add method in ArrayList can be given:
```csharp
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using System;

public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [DllImport("msvcrt.dll", CallingConvention = CallingConvention.Cdecl)]
    static extern int rand();

    [MethodUnderTest]
    public void PUT_AddContract([PexArgumentRange(0, 10)]int number)
    {
        // Arrange
        ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();

        // Act
        arrList.Add(number);

        // Assert
        int index = arrList.IndexOf(number);
        Assert.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);
    }

    static ArrayListContractTest()
    {
        // Initialize random seed
        rand();
    }
}
```

This dynamic test will try a wide range of random inputs (between 0 and 10) for the Add method under test. The PexArgumentRange attribute is used here to restrict the inputs to a small range for the sake of the example, but in real applications, much larger inputs may be more appropriate. Note that the test-generation method also includes combinations that test both regular and edge case scenarios, which should be representative of the vast range of possible inputs your code might encounter under normal operation and on the boundaries of your parameter space.

The rand() function call in the static constructor is simply to ensure that the random number generator is initialized before the tests are--------------------            Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
        }

    }
}
```

> [AZURE.WARNING] If you use the [Obsolete] attribute for the `PUT_InsertContract` method, you will not get any error. The Pex team has ignored this attribute for the purposes of contract testing.

### Testing the Order and Contents of the ArrayList Contract 
You will notice that the test harness provided more detailed assertions and observations. Specifically, note the `Assert` call at the end (in the debug section). It checks whether the position of `x` in the new list is not only greater or equal to the position in the old list, but also ensures that the elements before `x` in the old list still appear in the same positions in the new list.

Additionally, the `Observe` method is a useful tool to visualize the state of the ArrayList at various points in the test to understand the expected behavior of the code under test.

### Testing the Other Contracts 
Testing the other contracts of the `ArrayList` class involves examining the preconditions and postconditions for methods such as Remove, RemoveAt, Clear, etc. You will need to review the precondition assertion to ensure that the input parameters are valid (e.g., the index is within the range). Similarly, observe the postcondition assertion to ensure that operations are performed as expected.

That being said, it is important to note the following:

- For some methods, such as `RemoveAt` or `Remove`, the contract tests might not make sense because the behavior of these methods depend on the specifics of the ArrayList implementation, which may go beyond the standard behaviors that can be tested through pre/post conditions.

- For other methods, the pre/post conditions are quite straightforward to understand and verify. 

> [AZURE.TIP] Remember that as a first step in automated contract testing, you should also conduct unit testings to ensure that each individual method is working correctly. Automated contract testing and unit testing are complementary when it comes to covering all aspects of a software program.


## Conclusions
The goal of automated contract testing is to find pathological (and otherwise invalid) cases that would make your program fail in some scenarios but not in others. This allows you to find edge cases which can help improve the robustness of your code. 

In case of statically typed language, we could use automated tools to generate tests for the pre-conditions and post-conditions of a method's contract. The generated test cases then can be run against an executable to produce the execution trace which can be used to validate certain properties.

## References
For more information about automated contract testing, here are a few references I found particularly helpful:

- [Automated Contract Testing of Component-Based Software](http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/people/simonpj/papers/contracts-oopsla10.pdf) (Simon J. Thompson et. al.)

- [Towards Automatic Verification of Component-Based Software](http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/people/simonpj/papers/autoref10.pdf) (E. Allen Emerson et. al.)

- [Automated testing of component-based software with the Microsoft FxCop analyzer](http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/hh203609.aspx) (Jonathan P. Pelley et. al.)

- [A Contract-Based Approach to Automated Testing for Java Code](http://www.brics.dk/automation/contract-based-testing-java.html) (Eric Torreborre)

- [Boyer, R., et al.: Concurrency Testing Java Programs. Journal of Automated Software Tools, 2012](http://www.jazz.net/articles/issue_2012_02/issue_2012_02.html)

- [Smets, E. and W. Visser, The Practice of Automated Contract Testing](https://resources.sei.cmu.edu/library/asset-view.cfm?assetid=36002)

- [Moggi, M.: Automated testing of Java code with JML and SPARK. Information and Software Technology, 2004](http://www.springerlink.com/content/g023842j757--------------------	    // assertion
	    // (arrList.IndexOf(x) >= Old_arrListIndexOfX)

            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX - Old_index == 1, "The input value is incorrect"); 

	    Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX, "Contains property is incorrect");

            Assert.IsFalse(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount, "Count property is not incrementing as expected");

        }
    }
}

The test passes and fails as expected, but the output window isn't displaying any additional information in the message part of the Faults view nor in the PexObserve/Observe window. No error messages from the test run either. 

I believe there could be a problem with the setup for Faults view. Is there someone who would have faced a similar issue or knows how to correct this? 

A: When you use PexObserve.ValueForViewing, the actual message that is displayed in the Faults view is based on the test run results, rather than the expected or actual results. So if your PexObserve messages don't show up in the Faults view, it could be because the test run results you're passing into PexObserve don't match the expected results.

In the PexObserve/Observe, variables like "$old_arrList_Count" or "$old_arrList_x" can only reflect what value the program had in those points in terms of the test data, not the actual result of the test case when it ran on those points.

The reason you are unable to see any PexObserve values in the Faults view is because while PexObserve values are being populated correctly in the Observe window, they are not being used to generate a fault in the context of the test execution as it is meant to. Faults are created based on the conditions that need to be met to test an assertion or predicate, not the result of computations or observable state.

So in essence, the PexObserve methods are just used to facilitate visual debugging/inspection while running the test and not directly related to the Faults display in Pex Test Runner.

For a detailed understanding, you may refer to this discussion in Pex Forum - Debug Observe section.

Try running the tests using Visual Studio Test Runner first, then inspect the output in Test Results Window to see the actual output of your methods.

You also may want to use Debug.WriteLine for debugging. To see the printed output, navigate to View -> Other Windows -> Output. Then, set Output Window to Display Output from: Debug Only, or select Debug Output from the dropdown in Output window options.

Overall, using Debug.Assert or Debug.WriteLine to write out important info may help to understand what the test is doing and why it fails.


A: The issue may be related to:

*

*Ensuring you have installed latest Visual Studio 2010 service packs. The Pex testing in VS2010 requires at least SP1

*Right toolbar having focus. It is possible a fault is being flagged as a verification fault if there are no verification failures.

*Installing Microsoft Visual studio 2010 service pack 1.

*If the first three items do not resolve the problem, you may have to ensure your code is compiled with the Pex Runtime.

*Cleared test results.

*Make sure Pex is working in MS Test Project and not in a Console Application

*To check the setup of Pex, navigate to Test -> Edit Test Settings -> Default Processor Architecture, make sure its set to x86, 32bit and NOT AnyCPU, 64bit.

*Close and reopen Visual studio and rerun the tests.

*If all above still doesn't help, consider running the tests again and filtering the test cases in test explorer to Pex tests and run them again.

*Also try cleaning and rebuilding your project before testing.

*Make sure that the project where you are testing is built in Debug configuration.

Try doing this and see for yourself.

